course: dictionary
lesson: 12808
title: The word ell
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Ell is a short word with three meanings, so context tells you which one you need. Look at the words around it. «She bought an ell of cloth to make new curtains.» Bought and cloth tell you that ell is a measure, not a letter.
explain: The first meaning is the name of the letter L. «The last letter in the word ball is ell.» Ball ends in L, so ell is the name for that letter. Swap in the letter name and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The third meaning is used in America. «The kitchen is in the ell at the back of the house.» The house has a part that sticks out and makes an L shape. That tells you ell is a part of a building.
fact: Ell has three meanings: the letter L, an old measure of length, and a part of a building in America.
fact: «Ell» is the name of the letter L.
fact: An ell is an old measure of cloth, about the length of an arm. In England it was about 45 inches, a little over a metre.
fact: The proverb «Give him an inch and he'll take an ell» means if you give someone a little, they will take a lot.
fact: Today people often say «a mile» instead of «an ell» in that proverb.
exercise: «She bought an ell of cloth to make a curtain.» What is an ell in this sentence? | A small garden shed | A letter of the alphabet | A kind of bird | *A measure of length, about the length of an arm
exercise: Which letter of the alphabet is called ell? | *L | T | M | Z
exercise: «The shop is in the ell at the back of the house, making an L shape.» What does ell mean here? | A measure of cloth | A letter of the alphabet | *A part of a building that sticks out at a right angle | A kind of tree
test: The proverb «Give him an inch and he'll take an ell» means: | Measuring is hard work | *If you give someone a little, they will take a lot | Give people an inch of space, always | Always measure twice before you cut
test: Tom says ell only ever means a letter. What is the best reply? | Ell means a very long road | *Tom is not quite right: ell is also an old cloth measure, and in America a part of a building | Tom is right, ell is only a letter | Tom is right, and ell is a kind of bird
exam: «The old tailor measured the cloth with an ell, about the length of his arm.» What was the ell used for? | Counting people | *Measuring cloth | Writing letters | Building walls
